WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.660
Settle in, this is unintended consequences, episode 22 for June 3, 2026.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:11.160
Today's profile, a policy that did the opposite of what its designers intended.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:18.160
On June 17, 1971, President Richard Nixon stood in the White House briefing room and

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.740
told the nation that drug abuse had become public enemy number one.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.820
The administration's plan was straightforward.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:31.060
Use federal law enforcement to cut supply and punish demand so that addiction would shrink

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.920
like any other criminal enterprise.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:38.620
Instead, the policy that followed produced the largest expansion of incarceration in

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.800
American history, the rise of industrial scale trafficking organizations, and addiction

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.700
rates that remained stubbornly close to their 1971 levels.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:53.520
Nixon and his advisors were responding to visible increases in heroin use among returning

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.080
Vietnam veterans and in urban neighborhoods during the late 1960s.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.160
They viewed the problem as a national security and public order issue rather than a diffuse

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:05.060
medical one.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:10.560
This view was consistent with the era's limited treatment infrastructure and the recent success

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.420
of targeted enforcement against organized crime.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:19.980
Domestic policy chief John Erlichman later recalled that the White House believed strong criminal

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:25.580
sanctions would reduce both the supply reaching American streets and the social disorder associated

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.120
with visible drug markets.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:33.440
The approach also aligned with the comprehensive drug abuse prevention and control act of 1970,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.060
which had already consolidated federal authority over narcotics.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:43.600
At the time, few models existed for scaling evidence-based treatment nationally, so criminal

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.480
prohibition appeared to be the most direct lever available to a president facing reelection

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.620
pressures over law and order concerns.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:57.820
Nixon formally announced the war on drugs in 1971 and created the special action office for

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:03.740
drug abuse prevention while simultaneously expanding the Bureau of Narcotics and Dangerous Drugs.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:10.600
In 1973, the Drug Enforcement Administration was established, consolidating enforcement

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:11.180
authority.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:18.740
Federal drug arrests rose from roughly 3,000 in 1970 to more than 10,000 within a few years.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.040
Early data showed temporary drops in street-level heroin purity in some cities and increased

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.980
seizures along the Mexican border.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.960
Proponents pointed to these metrics as proof that supply could be constricted.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.740
Skeptics inside the public health community warned that enforcement would simply raise

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:39.860
prices and profits for whoever remained in the trade.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.880
Those cautions received limited attention amid bipartisan support for tougher sentencing.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:54.380
By the 1980s and 1990s, mandatory minimum laws and the anti-drug abuse acts of 1986 and

00:02:54.380 --> 00:03:02.780
1988 drove state and federal prison populations from approximately 200,000 in 1970 to more than

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.040
1.2 million by 2000.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:11.460
The black market premium created by prohibition generated revenues that allowed Colombian and

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:17.260
later Mexican trafficking groups to professionalize into multinational enterprises with private

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.280
armies and diversified logistics.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.140
Domestic enforcement pressure displaced rather than eliminated production, shifting cultivation

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:30.900
from Turkey and Mexico to more remote regions, and later to fentanyl precursors from China.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:35.020
Families and communities experienced concentrated effects.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.920
By 2010, roughly 1 in 9 black children had a parent who had been incarcerated, most often

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.480
for non-violent drug offenses.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.800
Treatment capacity lagged because federal funding priorities remained tilted toward interdiction.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:56.060
Estimates from the era suggest that for every dollar spent on treatment, roughly 10 went

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:56.760
to enforcement.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.380
Second order effects included the militarization of local police departments through asset

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:07.260
forfeiture programs, and the erosion of trust between law enforcement and the neighborhoods

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:08.840
most heavily policed.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.020
Cartels adapted faster than agencies could respond, turning enforcement pressure into a barrier

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:18.700
to entry that rewarded only the most sophisticated organizations.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:25.820
By the 2010s, states began rolling back some mandatory minimum statutes and expanding drug

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:30.240
courts and treatment access, while several jurisdictions legalized cannabis.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:36.420
The Obama and Trump administrations each reduced the federal crack cocaine sentencing disparity

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:39.980
and redirected modest additional funds toward treatment.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:47.560
Erlichman's 1994 interview remarks published in 2016 crystallized retrospective criticism

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.920
from inside the original policy circle.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:55.520
Nevertheless, the core architecture of prohibition and aggressive supply side enforcement

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:57.620
persists at the federal level.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.620
New unintended consequences have appeared.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:06.860
The rapid spread of fentanyl, a drug far easier to conceal than plant-based narcotics, has

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:12.940
driven overdose deaths above 70,000 annually, even as incarceration rates have begun to decline.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:19.360
Some localities that abruptly reduced enforcement without simultaneous treatment expansion saw

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.780
open-air markets reemerge, illustrating how partial reforms can create fresh gaps.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.560
When a policy defines a complex behavioral and medical problem primarily as a criminal

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:36.460
supply issue, the resulting incentive structure rewards those who can operate most effectively

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:37.660
outside the law.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:42.060
Complex social phenomena resist single-lever interventions.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:47.720
Enforcement pressure tends to concentrate profits among the most adaptable actors rather

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:49.360
than eliminating the activity.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:55.080
Decision makers today can test whether proposed solutions address root demand and whether

00:05:55.080 --> 00:06:00.280
enforcement mechanisms contain built-in adaptation advantages for illicit networks.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:07.680
How my current regulatory or enforcement proposals in other domains, housing, migration, or digital

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.920
platforms shift incentives in ways that reward the very behaviors they aim to suppress?

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:15.640
That's unintended consequences for today.

